	)
	(text "OUTPUT STAGE, DERIVED FROM BOTH VERIFIED ENDPOINTS -- neither end assumed.\n  AK4458 full scale = 2.8 Vpp differential = 0.9899 Vrms   (V-05, at AVDD 5 V)\n  S6.2 [HARD]       = 4.5 Vrms before clip into 10 kOhm\n  -> required gain  = 4.546 x = 13.15 dB\n  Rin = 2.21k, Rf = 10.2k per leg -> ACTUAL gain 4.615 x, output 4.569 Vrms (+0.13 dB vs 4.5)\n\nV-06 CLOSES, AND THE [HARD] LINE CLEARS ON THE DATASHEET'S NUMBER:\n  OPA1656 output swing = (V-)+0.25 .. (V+)-0.25, i.e. +/-13.25 V on +/-13V5_A rails.\n  4.5 Vrms needs 6.36 V peak.  13.25 / 6.36 = 2.08x -> 6.4 dB of headroom.\n  The swing spec is quoted at RL = 2 kOhm; S6.2 loads 10 kOhm, i.e. LIGHTER, so the\n  real margin is at least this. NOT marginal, and not assumed.\n\nV-06 POSTSCRIPT (F48's question asked of this stage): OPA1656 input abs max is\n  (V-)-0.5 .. (V+)+0.5 = +/-14.0 V, the same form that caught the input sheet. Here it\n  is NOT at risk: the op-amp inputs sit inside the feedback loop, driven only by the\n  AK4458's outputs, which are bounded by its own AVDD (5 V). THE MUTE ELEMENTS ARE\n  DOWNSTREAM OF THE OUTPUT PIN, so no mute transient reaches an input. The one exposed\n  pin is OUT, and its abs max is 'output short-circuit: continuous'.\n\nFILTER: 150 pF across Rf gives a pole at 104 kHz -- far above the 19+20 kHz CCIF\ntwin-tone pair S12 tests this stage with, so the criterion measures the STAGE, not the\nfilter's skirt. A pole placed for 'niceness' near 20 kHz would corrupt that measurement."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 560.07 39.37 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "07b84adf-6b75-5224-a27d-8bc84dd2256d")
	)
	(text "MUTE: SERIES-SHUNT, AND THE FAIL-SAFE WALK IS THE WHOLE POINT (S6.4 / S7.4 [HARD]).\nOrder along the signal path is SERIES FIRST, THEN SHUNT, THEN build-out to the jack.\n\n  K21-K26  Form A photoMOS, ENERGISED-TO-PASS. LED from MUTE_EN through 1k.\n  Q21-Q26  depletion pair, BACK-TO-BACK SOURCE-TO-SOURCE, gate biased off from\n            -13V5_A through a 100k/100k divider.\n\nTHE WALK -- each element fails safe in its OWN polarity, and the depth is the PRODUCT:\n  * Rails collapse (crank sag, blown fuse, pulled connector -- the S7.4 cases, none of\n    which involve the MCU):\n      +5V_D dies  -> LED current stops  -> SERIES OPENS. Signal path broken.\n      -13V5_A dies -> gate bias dies    -> Vgs -> 0 -> DEPLETION DEVICE CONDUCTS\n                                        -> SHUNT CLOSES. Residual shorted to ground.\n    NEITHER needs firmware, a sequence, or a rail that is still alive.\n  * A SINGLE depletion FET would RECTIFY (its body diode conducts one polarity), which\n    is why the pair is source-to-source: two body diodes back to back block both ways.\n  * ENHANCEMENT devices are excluded BY CONSTRUCTION -- they need Vgs to conduct, so a\n    dead rail leaves the shunt OPEN, which is the opposite of what is wanted.\n\n*** V-14 and V-15 ARE BOUNDED HERE, NOT CLOSED. *** K and Q are PLACEHOLDER SYMBOLS:\nthe TOPOLOGY is drawn and netlist-checkable, the PART NUMBERS are not pinned, because\ntheir datasheets are not held and CLAUDE.md s8 rule 9 has no exception for a part\nwhose function is obvious. What the parts must satisfy:\n  V-14 series Ron: Zout budget is <= 100 Ohm (S6.2). Build-out is 10R, so\n        Ron(photoMOS) <= 100 - 10R = 90 Ohm, AND its Ron must be flat with current --\n        a resistance that varies with signal IS distortion, measured by the same CCIF\n        criterion. THE SERIES ELEMENT IS NOW INSIDE THE OUTPUT-IMPEDANCE BUDGET; it\n        was not when S6.2 was written.\n  V-15 shunt Coff: off-capacitance and its VOLTAGE COEFFICIENT. This is the mechanism\n        S12's CCIF twin-tone line exists to catch -- both 1 kHz and 20 kHz THD are\n        blind to it.\n  V-16 depth: DAC residual x mute attenuation x amp gain x amp mute latency. Bound,\n        not closed: remote-out is fail-safe and drops FIRST, so the window is tens to\n        a couple hundred ms of someone else's amplifier, and the residual is a\n        brown-out-resetting DAC, not programme material. Bench-refined."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 560.07 180.34 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "f4ad3119-a4bb-51ad-b65e-e98ff14dd6b1")
	)
	(text "CHANNEL NUMBERING -- THE ONE PLACE THE IDENTITY DELIBERATELY BREAKS (F10, S10 [HARD]).\n  This device is AK4458 #2. Its OWN outputs are L1 R1 L2 R2 L3 R3 (slots 1-6).\n  They carry SYSTEM channels AOUT7..AOUT12.\n  AOUTn <-> (device ceil(n/6), slot ((n-1) mod 6)+1)\nSo on THIS sheet the net names and the device's own pin names do not agree, and that\nis correct: S10 says net names follow the SYSTEM channel number, never the per-device\nslot. Written here, at the wire, because this is the only sheet where the two differ\nand the temptation to 'fix' the mismatch lives exactly here.\n\nSLOTS 7-8 RESERVED [HARD]: L4/R4 (pins 38/39/43/42) are EXPLICIT NO-CONNECTS. T1-52 is\ncounter-intuitive and specific -- unused ANALOG outputs must be left OPEN, while unused\nDIGITAL inputs on the same part (SDTI2-4, DSDR3, DSDL4, DSDR4) must be tied to DVSS.\nOpposite treatments, same device, and DZF must also be left open."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 560.07 400.05 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "c53c508a-f7da-5d99-93c0-0df1ba407cd6")
	)
	(embedded_fonts no)
)
